{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015WE7VmxE24t8e651GDixKG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the full experiment plan. Let me identify all the \"planned\" experiments and generate detailed median-tier predictions for each.\n\nLooking at the experiment plan, the planned/running experiments are:\n\n1. plan_001 - cancelled\n2. plan_002 - blocked\n3. plan_003 - planned (LoRA baseline eval, 2 seeds)\n4. plan_004 - running (LoRA baseline training, but listed as DONE in system prompt)\n5. plan_005 - running (Phase 2 λ=0 ablation)\n\n\n6. plan_006 - planned (Phase 2 λ=0 eval)\n7. plan_007 - running (spatial_bowl A1 training)\n8. plan_008 - planned (spatial_bowl eval)\n9. plan_009 - running (Lambda=0.1 bracket training)\n10. plan_010 - planned (Lambda=0.1 eval)\n11. plan_011 - cancelled\n12. plan_012 - planned (Phase 2 additional seed)\n13. plan_013 - planned (LoRA baseline seed2)\n14. plan_014 - planned (dfCAR threshold sensitivity)\n15. plan_015 - planned (spatial_bowl LoRA)\n\nNow I'm working through the median-tier predictions for each experiment. SDF redundancy appears stable under normal conditions but weakens with high lambda or Phase 2 settings, so I need to be more cautious with my claims. The LoRA seed2 results show slightly lower CAR around 48-53%, which would push the 3-seed mean to about 53% with increased variance, though the SDF delta stays near zero. The 3v3 comparison is borderline—the delta sits between -2 and +5 percentage points with p-values around 0.1-0.3, so I can't confidently say the results are indistinguishable. Phase 2 shows CAR slightly higher than LoRA at 58-65%, suggesting SDF might have a weak persistent effect, but the true success rate remains low at 10-25%. For Lambda=0.1, I'm expecting CAR in the 56-62% range.\n\nAt extreme lambda values, a weak dose-response pattern emerges where SDF shows only marginal effects. The spatial_bowl experiment lacks a proper LoRA control, which prevents me from doing cross-scene SDF attribution since plan_015 is incomplete. The dfCAR threshold appears stable and unaffected by other claims. I'm tracking several key metric ranges across these experiments to establish the boundaries of what the data can support.\n\nNow I'm working through the experiment timeline. Plan_003 is labeled as LoRA baseline evaluation with 2 seeds, but the registry already shows those evaluations are complete. There's a discrepancy between the YAML status showing \"planned\" and the actual registry entries showing they're done. The system prompt's paper_framing.md appears more current since it references D025 findings that include LoRA baseline results, suggesting the YAML status might be outdated.\n\nFor this task, I need to focus on experiments marked as \"planned\" in the YAML—that's plan_003, plan_006, plan_008, plan_010, plan_012, plan_013, plan_014, and plan_015. Some like plan_013 show \"running\" status in the registry despite being marked \"planned\" in the YAML, so there's some inconsistency to work through.\n\nLooking at the dependencies, plan_003 depends on plan_004 (LoRA training), which is already marked DONE in the system registry. The LoRA evaluations have been completed, so plan_003 is essentially finished. For the remaining pending experiments, I should generate median-tier predictions as the user requested. For the remaining experiments, I'll generate median-tier imagined results. Plan_003 is already complete with real data showing LoRA baseline performance around 55% CAR across two seeds, which aligns with the median tier expectations. Plan_004 is also done, so I'll move through the rest systematically with consistent median-tier metrics.\n\nFor the Phase 2 ablation with λ=0, I'm expecting CAR to improve slightly to around 62% with TSR around 18%, while the spatial_bowl experiment should show lower performance at roughly 35% CAR. The λ=0.1 bracket training is currently running and should complete with median-tier results around that performance range.\n\nLooking at the additional experiments, I'm projecting the Phase 2 seed results to cluster around 59-60% CAR with low TSR, while the LoRA baseline's third seed should land around 49% CAR to bring the three-seed mean down to approximately 53%. The dfCAR threshold sensitivity analysis suggests the metric remains stable across different threshold values for both LoRA and SDF models.\n\nFor the A1 model specifically, dfCAR shows essentially no improvement across any threshold. The spatial_bowl LoRA-only control experiment is still pending, but I'd expect it to achieve roughly 30% CAR if it follows the SDF pattern. Now I'm organizing these results into a structured YAML format and reviewing the rationale for each experiment—the LoRA baseline results demonstrate that fine-tuning on collision-free demonstrations produces conservative behavior that achieves around 55% CAR without relying on geometric constraints.\n\nWhen I look at the phase 2 ablation where SDF loss is removed after 10K steps of SDF training, the results show CAR improves to 62% with TSR climbing to 18%. This suggests the SDF training leaves some residual effect on the model's learned representations—the model partially recovers task performance when the loss is removed, but remains significantly impaired compared to the 73% baseline, indicating the conservative bias persists even after removing the geometric supervision.\n\nMoving to spatial_bowl with SDF+LoRA, performance drops sharply to CAR=35% and TSR=3%, which appears driven more by the scene's inherent difficulty with constrained movements near obstacles rather than SDF effectiveness—without a LoRA-only baseline for this scene, I can't cleanly separate the two factors.\n\nAt λ=0.1, the stronger penalty pushes the model toward more conservative behavior, with CAR stabilizing around 59% across seeds while TSR remains low at 4%, suggesting the amplified SDF weight creates a more pronounced